<h1>Likutay Halachos — Yoreh De'ah, Volume I</h1>
<h2>Hilchos Traifos, Halachah 1</h2>

<p class="intro">
Based on the Torah [discourse] "Tefillah LaChavakuk" in Siman 19, which is referenced above in Hilchos Shechitah, Halachah 2; see there.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 1</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span></strong> <span class="gloss">[categories of organic defects in an animal's vital organs that render it non-kosher, lit. "torn"]</span>. For they are the aspect of <span class="key-term">tikkun habris</span> <span class="gloss">[rectification of the holy Covenant]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="verse">"Tzadik Chai Almin"</span> <span class="gloss">[the Righteous One Who sustains the worlds]</span>. For a <span class="key-term">traifah</span> does not live, and on account of this it is forbidden to eat, as it is written: <span class="verse">"This is the living creature [<em>zos hachayah</em>] that you may eat"</span> <span class="source">(Vayikra 11:2)</span>. As our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <em>"That which lives [<span class="key-term">chayah</span>] you may eat; that which does not live you may not eat."</em> For the <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> <span class="gloss">[the side of death]</span> rests upon it, which is the aspect of the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"zos hachayah"</span> — <span class="verse">"zos"</span> specifically, the aspect of the completeness of <span class="key-term">lashon hakodesh</span> [the Holy Tongue], which is the aspect of <span class="verse">zos</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">"and this [<em>v'zos</em>] is what he spoke"</span> <span class="source">(Devarim 33:1)</span> <span class="source">(as Rabbeinu said there)</span>. That is, Yisrael are only permitted to eat <span class="verse">"zos hachayah"</span> specifically — that which is from the <span class="key-term">sitra d'chayai</span> <span class="gloss">[the side of life]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="verse">zos</span>, the aspect of the completeness of <span class="key-term">lashon hakodesh</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">tikkun habris</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore, it is forbidden to eat the eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span>, in order to merit the aspect of life, the aspect of <span class="verse">"Tzadik Chai Almin,"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is written <span class="source">(Shemos 22:30)</span>: <span class="verse">"And flesh torn in the field [<em>traifah</em>] you shall not eat; to the dog you shall cast it."</span> <span class="verse">"To the dog"</span> specifically — for to it is given over the aspect of the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">"The leech has two daughters: 'Give! Give!'"</span> <span class="source">(Mishlei 30:15)</span>, as Rabbeinu wrote in the discourse <em>"Hatzilah MeCherev Nafshi, MiYad Kelev Yechidasi"</em> <span class="source">(Siman 50)</span>. For when the animal is slaughtered properly through the aspect of the above-mentioned "sword," which is the aspect of the completeness of <span class="key-term">lashon hakodesh</span>, and it is found kosher upon inspection — that it has no <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> <span class="gloss">[defect rendering it <em>traif</em>]</span> whatsoever — then it is in the aspect of a <span class="key-term">korban</span> [offering], as mentioned above. And then it is in the aspect of: <em>"the lion descends to eat and consumes the offering"</em> <span class="gloss">[<span class="key-term">aryeh nachis l'maichal v'achail kurbana</span>; i.e. the holy "lion" on the <span class="key-term">mizbe'ach</span> consumes it]</span>. But when the animal is found to be <span class="key-term">traif</span> and the <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> rests upon it — which is the aspect of the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> — then it is fit for the dog specifically, as mentioned above, in the aspect of <em>"the dog descends"</em> <span class="gloss">[<span class="key-term">kalba nachis</span>; the unholy counterpart]</span>, which is the opposite of the "double-edged sword" of holiness, as mentioned above. For it is the aspect of the <em>marah</em> <span class="gloss">[bile; the gallbladder]</span> that has "two mouths," etc.; see there.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is written regarding <span class="key-term">traifos</span> as well: <span class="verse">"And you shall be men of holiness to Me; and flesh torn in the field [<em>traifah</em>],"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Shemos 22:30)</span>. For it is the aspect of holiness, the aspect of guarding the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as mentioned above. And this is why all the <span class="key-term">traifos</span> are subsumed in the Shulchan Aruch under <strong>eight categories of <span class="key-term">traifos</span></strong> — corresponding to the aspect of <span class="key-term">tikkun habris</span>, which is the aspect of the eighth [<span class="key-term">sheminis</span>], as mentioned above <span class="source">(in the above-mentioned Laws of Shechitah)</span>. And this is why, in their details, there are found <strong>seventy <span class="key-term">traifos</span></strong>, as the Rambam enumerated them <span class="source">(Hilchos Shechitah 10:9)</span> — corresponding to the seventy nations. And one must guard against the seventy <span class="key-term">traifos</span> in order to be saved from the encompassing evil of the above-mentioned seventy nations.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 2</div>

<p>
And therefore, the essential <span class="key-term">traifah</span> depends upon the <strong>lungs [<span class="key-term">rai'ah</span>]</strong>. For there resides the essential <em>ruach</em> [wind/spirit] that cools the heat, through which the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> [knowledge/awareness] occurs <span class="source">(as is brought in the words of Rabbeinu, Likutay Moharan Part II, 8, 12)</span>. And the rectification of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> is the rectification of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore, the five lobes — the lungs have [lobes] corresponding to the Five Books of the Torah <span class="source">(as Rabbeinu said)</span> — corresponding to the <em>heh</em> <span class="gloss">[the Hebrew letter ה, numerical value five]</span> of <span class="key-term">da'as</span>, for there the essential <span class="key-term">da'as</span> depends, as mentioned above. And therefore, one does not need to inspect any other [organ] for <span class="key-term">tarfus</span>; only the lungs require inspection, because <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> is commonly found there <span class="source">(as is written in the Shulchan Aruch)</span>. For this is a principle: the essential intensification of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achara</span> <span class="gloss">[the "Other Side," i.e. the forces of impurity]</span> is only in a place where there is extra holiness. And therefore, in the lungs — where the essential <em>ruach chayyim</em> [spirit of life] and the sustaining of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> reside — there they intensify themselves to cause <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> in it. And therefore, <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> is commonly found there.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, <strong>if the spleen [<span class="key-term">t'chol</span>] is removed, [the animal] is kosher</strong>. For the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> is the opposite of the lungs. The <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> suckles from the murkiness of the blood, from the derangement of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> <span class="gloss">[<em>tairuf hada'as</em>]</span>, and it is the <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> [side of death]. Therefore, the vitality does not depend upon it, and therefore [the animal] is kosher if it is removed. For <span class="key-term">traifah</span> is the aspect of the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, the flaw of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span>, as mentioned above. And on account of this it is called <span class="key-term">traifah</span>, from the language of <em>tairuf hada'as</em> [derangement of the mind]. For <span class="key-term">traifah</span> is the aspect of the flaw of eating, from which the foolishness and the derangement of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> mentioned in the discourse <em>"VaYehi Heim Merikim Sakeihem"</em> <span class="source">(Siman 17)</span> suckle — and from there the idolatry suckles <span class="source">(as is explained there)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, whose essential suckling and intensification is through the derangement of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> mentioned above. For the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> is the foolishness, the aspect of <span class="verse">"the laughter of the fool"</span> <span class="source">(Koheles 7:6)</span> — idolatry, <em>eil acher</em> <span class="gloss">[a foreign god]</span> — as is found in the holy Zohar and is brought in the discourse <em>"Tzivisa Tzedek"</em> <span class="source">(Siman 23)</span>. For the essential intensification of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> is through the flaw of eating, as it is written: <span class="verse">"in sorrow shall you eat of it"</span> <span class="source">(Bereishis 3:17)</span>. This is the aspect of the intensification of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, which is sadness and <span class="key-term">marah shechorah</span> <span class="gloss">[black bile, i.e. melancholy]</span>, idolatry, <em>anpin chashuchin</em> <span class="gloss">[darkened countenance]</span>, sadness and bitterness of livelihood <span class="source">(as all of this is explained in the discourse <em>"Tzivisa Tzedek"</em> mentioned above)</span>. And all of this comes about through the flaw of eating. For <span class="verse">"in sorrow shall you eat of it"</span> was decreed on account of the flaw of eating, as it is written: <span class="verse">"Have you eaten of the tree from which I commanded you not to eat?"</span> <span class="source">(Bereishis 3:11)</span>. And through this was decreed <span class="verse">"in sorrow shall you eat of it"</span> — which is the intensification of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For conversely, when the eating is in a kosher manner — that is, when one guards oneself from <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> — then it is the subduing of the foolishness, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, as mentioned above. And then there is livelihood in abundance, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"He has given sustenance [<em>teref</em>] to those who fear Him"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 111:5)</span>, as Rabbeinu wrote <span class="source">(in Siman 17)</span>. That is, when Yisrael guard themselves from <span class="key-term">traifah</span>, then <span class="verse">"He has given sustenance,"</span> etc. For the essential livelihood depends upon the completeness of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> and the guarding of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> <span class="source">(as Rabbeinu wrote in several places)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, most types of <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> — such as perforation, severance, and removal — <strong>do not disqualify in the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span></strong>, for the vitality does not depend upon it. For on the contrary, it is the <span class="key-term">sitra d'mosa</span> — foolishness, idolatry. However, nevertheless, some <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> does apply to it — namely, that a small amount of it must remain if it was perforated at <em>somchei'h</em> <span class="gloss">[its supporting tissue, i.e. its thick part]</span> — that is, the thickness of a gold <span class="key-term">dinar</span>. For the foolishness, too, is part of the sustaining of the edifice [of the world], and one must give it some suckling and vitality in a contracted measure as well, as is brought. And as is implied in the discourse <em>"VaYehi Heim"</em> mentioned above — that this is the sustaining of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span>: when one gives the foolishness some suckling as well, only that it should be in a great contraction — just enough for its sustenance. Therefore, a small minimum of vitality depends upon the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, which is the foolishness, and a small amount of it must remain — just enough for its sustenance, in a contracted measure.
</p>

<p>
And this is what our Sages of blessed memory measured: that its [minimum] measure of vitality is <strong>the thickness of a gold <span class="key-term">dinar</span></strong>. For from there is its suckling and vitality, in the aspect of <span class="verse">"and dust of gold"</span> <span class="source">(Iyov 28:6)</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="verse">"and dust you shall eat all the days of your life"</span> <span class="source">(Bereishis 3:14)</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span>, which is the sadness and worries of livelihood — <span class="verse">"in sorrow shall you eat of it"</span> — mentioned above. This is the aspect of <span class="verse">"the laughter of the fool"</span> — namely, the craving for money <span class="source">(as all of this is brought in the holy Zohar and explained well in the discourse <em>"Tzivisa Tzedek"</em> mentioned above)</span>: <em>"Earth is cold and dry; so too the spleen,"</em> etc.; see there. And the principle is: the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> is the aspect of the craving for money, which is the aspect of "dust of gold," the aspect of foolishness, idolatry <span class="source">(as is explained there)</span>. And even though this is the opposite of vitality, nevertheless a small amount of it must remain for its sustenance in a contracted measure — for one must give vitality even to the foolishness, and it too is part of the sustaining of the edifice, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <em>ruach tzafon</em> <span class="gloss">[the northern wind/spirit]</span> that was left lacking in the building of the world — in order to give them a place, for there is their place and their vitality, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"from the north shall evil break forth"</span> <span class="source">(Yirmeyahu 1:14)</span>. And this is the aspect of "dust of gold" mentioned above, as it is written: <span class="verse">"from the north gold comes"</span> <span class="source">(Iyov 37:22)</span>. And therefore, the <span class="key-term">korbanos</span> [offerings] that were brought in order to subdue the foolishness, as mentioned above — nevertheless, the aspect of foolishness would also receive vitality from the <span class="key-term">mizbe'ach</span> [altar] <span class="source">(as Rabbeinu wrote there)</span>. And this is the smoke of the <span class="key-term">korbanos</span>, as is brought. And therefore, the smoke would bend toward the north side, in order to give them a portion — the aspect of <span class="verse">"from the north shall evil break forth,"</span> etc.; the aspect of <span class="verse">"from the north gold."</span> And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">t'chol</span> — the aspect of "dust of gold," foolishness, etc. And one must give it, too, its sustenance in a contracted measure, as mentioned above.
</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>Translator's Summary</h3>

  <p>
    <em>Traifos 1</em> is a compact two-section halachah built upon <strong>Likutay Moharan I:19</strong> (<em>"Tefillah LaChavakuk"</em>). Its axis is <strong><em>tikkun habris</em></strong> (rectification of the Covenant) and its relationship to kosher eating.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Section 1:</strong> The eighteen <em>traifos</em> = <em>tikkun habris</em> = <em>Tzadik Chai Almin</em>. A <em>traifah</em> does not live; the side of death rests upon it = the flaw of the <em>bris</em>. <em>"Zos hachayah"</em> = the completeness of <em>lashon hakodesh</em>. A properly slaughtered, kosher animal = a <em>korban</em> consumed by the "lion" of holiness; a <em>traifah</em> is given to the "dog." Eight categories of <em>traifos</em> correspond to <em>sheminis</em> (<em>tikkun habris</em>); seventy detailed <em>traifos</em> (per the Rambam) correspond to the seventy nations.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Section 2:</strong> <em>Tarfus</em> mainly affects the lungs — seat of the cooling <em>ruach</em> and thus the <em>da'as</em>. The five lobes = five Books of Torah = the <em>heh</em> of <em>da'as</em>. The <em>Sitra Achara</em> attacks wherever holiness is greatest, hence <em>tarfus</em> is common in the lungs. The spleen (<em>t'chol</em>), by contrast, is the opposite: it suckles from murkiness, foolishness, <em>marah shechorah</em>, idolatry, and the craving for money. Removing it does not render the animal <em>traif</em>. Yet a minimum must remain ("thickness of a gold <em>dinar</em>"), because even the foolishness plays a role in sustaining the world's edifice — like the <em>ruach tzafon</em> left open in creation. The smoke of the <em>korbanos</em> bending northward = giving the <em>kelipah</em> its contracted measure of sustenance. When eating is kosher, the <em>t'chol</em>/foolishness is subdued, and livelihood flows: <em>"teref nasan li'rai'av."</em>
  </p>
</div>


